TERM SHEET SUNDAY
Sunday, March 15, 2026

The week in venture, for founders who read the documents.

Q1, WITH TWO WEEKS LEFT
Dollars are tracking flat year on year; deal count is down about 15%. Bigger checks into fewer companies, concentrated in AI infrastructure and in vertical software with a hardware or plant-floor component. Generalist funds are writing fewer Series A checks and more Series B checks into companies the specialists found. If you are raising an A from a generalist right now, expect a longer process and a lower valuation than the specialist would offer.

THE INDEPENDENT DIRECTOR PROBLEM
Almost every Series A term sheet calls for an independent board seat. Almost nobody fills it before the round closes, and about a third of the companies in our sample had not filled it a year later. The seat matters most in exactly the situations founders do not anticipate: a down round, a founder dispute, an acquisition offer. Fill it in the first ninety days, with someone who has run a company at the stage you are going to be at, not the stage you are at.

READING A SIDE LETTER
Side letters are where the terms the lead did not want in the main documents go. Common ones: most-favored-nation on future terms, a right to co-invest in future rounds beyond pro-rata, a consent right over a specific decision (a sale, a new office, a pivot). Read them as carefully as the charter, because they bind the company, and ask that every side letter be disclosed to every investor. A side letter one investor does not know about is a lawsuit waiting for a bad quarter.

ROUNDS THIS WEEK
- A plant-floor analytics company raised a $28M Series A led by a specialist industrial fund, with a well-known generalist following.
- A cold-chain telemetry rollup raised debt, not equity. Watch for more of that.
- The largest seed fund in the Bay Area closed its new vehicle a third smaller than the last one, by choice.

See you next Sunday.

